0e739" :question/math-academy-id "q-2464" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+4x+4.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =4x+4 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ has no real solutions, and so there are no points of inflection." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-2464/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-2464"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-21035/selection/592b9f18cf04a8c75d513e20b1b4a536540e9c3c0f5c7614ffaf1943dc6c7d15" :answer/id #uuid "0cd4ab4f-95a1-5b0c-80a0-5c09c687f7aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21035/selection/4e6eeef5aa33ee4a1d2fff0c2887be5f32fe5006030d9cd9500bd4b039270d64" :answer/id #uuid "ee1fd109-16af-5879-bcfd-4d1366864d1f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21035/selection/264ed6922279f92bdc90c36a26ea47de8f6c6ab903ed9e63c8b8bb51a5a9d4f6" :answer/id #uuid "adef789f-225b-5894-a8b9-5e11934c377c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21035/selection/84ea79387d51fde1e84146322c8639e6e5eae9a28be16c7c74f23a3fe78716de" :answer/id #uuid "abf597b9-e766-597c-b9d7-6faf4a80df03" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There are no points of inflection" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21035/selection/cb1896197a67f182a612a87dbfdf77a8994e499a7df0036f929b0112dee816e0" :answer/id #uuid "6d418e2c-fc04-5c80-b390-b1fd